  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "ketogenics protein powder",
      "ketogenics zero carb zero sugar keto protein powder",
      "ketogenics iso-whey whey protein isolate keto friendly protein powder",
      "ketogenics zero carb protein",
      "ketogenics iso whey",
      "ketoshop protein powder",
      "keto protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Low Carb Picks",
    link: "/best-low-carb-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Ketogenics Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      What is on the labels of KetoGenics Zero Carb and ISO-WHEY, whether either is third-party tested, what each 25 g of protein costs, and which Amazon powders come closest.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#zero-carb">Zero Carb Label</a>
      <a href="#iso-whey">ISO-WHEY Label</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Tested?</a>
      <a href="#zero-carb-keto">Really Zero Carb?</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyers">Taste and Complaints</a>
      <a href="#vs-isopure">vs Isopure Zero Carb</a>
      <a href="#who">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          KetoGenics Zero Carb is a genuinely zero-carb, stevia-sweetened whey and casein blend with 22 g of protein per scoop for about $2.03 per 25 g, but it has no third-party certification and sells only on the brand's site. ISO-WHEY adds protein but, in flavored versions, sucralose. Below: both labels, testing, cost, buyer feedback and Amazon alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Ketogenics Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            KetoGenics makes a genuinely zero-carb protein powder with a short, stevia-sweetened label, and at about $2.03 per 25 g of protein it is fairly priced, but it carries no third-party certification and you can only buy it on the brand's own site. Its flagship, <strong>Zero Carb, Zero Sugar Keto Protein Powder</strong>, gives 22 g of protein, 100 calories and 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop, per the label at the time of writing. The second product, <strong>ISO-WHEY</strong>, gives 25 g of protein but uses sucralose in the flavored versions we checked.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are on keto or low carb, you want stevia rather than sucralose, and you like a thicker shake you can also bake with. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you need an NSF or Informed Choice certified powder, you want the most protein per scoop, or you would rather order through Amazon. For the full low-carb field, see our <a href='/best-low-carb-protein-powder/'>best low carb protein powder</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Zero Carb (Chocolate Milkshake)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">ISO-WHEY (Chocolate Cereal Milk)</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein per scoop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">115</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1.5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein source</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate + micellar casein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whey isolate</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweetener</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Servings / price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">28 / $49.99</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 / $49.99</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Cost per 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.03</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.08</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Third-party certified</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Label figures are per the KetoGenics supplement-facts panels at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Prices are the brand-site prices we saw on November 21, 2026, before shipping.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Both KetoGenics Proteins and Three Amazon Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            The first two cards are the KetoGenics products this review covers; they are sold on KetoShop.com, not Amazon, so their prices are brand-site prices before shipping. The next three are the closest powders you can buy on Amazon. All prices are the ones we saw on November 21, 2026, and they change often.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="zero-carb" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Ketogenics Zero Carb Zero Sugar Keto Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            KetoGenics Zero Carb is a blend of whey protein isolate and micellar casein, sweetened only with stevia, with 22 g of protein and 0 g of carbohydrate in each 27.1 g scoop of Chocolate Milkshake, per the label at the time of writing. The brand describes the blend as 80% whey isolate and 20% micellar casein. The bag itself is printed "Zero Carb Natural Protein", while the store lists it as "Zero Carb, Zero Sugar Keto Protein Powder", so both names refer to the same product.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the Chocolate Milkshake panel is just as lean: 100 calories, 0 g of fat, 0 g of total sugars, 0 g of added sugar, 0 g of sugar alcohol and 0 g of fiber, with 115 mg of sodium, 106 mg of calcium and 115 mg of potassium. There are 28 servings in a 1.67 lb (758.8 g) bag. The printed ingredient list is whey protein isolate, micellar casein, natural flavors, sunflower lecithin, cocoa, stevia and sea salt. The only declared allergen is milk.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Unflavored/Unsweetened version is even simpler. Per its label, a 25 g scoop gives the same 22 g of protein and 100 calories with only three ingredients: whey protein isolate, micellar casein and sunflower lecithin. That makes it the cleanest option in the whole KetoGenics range for people who want to add protein to coffee, soups or baking without any sweetener.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the casein matters</h3>

          <p>
            Micellar casein digests more slowly than whey and thickens liquids. That is why KetoGenics pitches Zero Carb for baking, protein "pudding" stirred into Greek yogurt, and a pre-bed shake. It also explains the slightly lower protein per scoop: a 27 g scoop delivering 22 g is about 81% protein by weight, which is good, and the brand's own Unflavored ISO-WHEY reaches about 90%, since it has nothing but isolate and lecithin. If you plan to bake, the blend is the more practical choice; if you only drink shakes after training, either works.
          </p>

          <p>
            The front of the bag also says lactose free, gluten free, aspartame free and soy free, and labels the product "keto friendly". The back carries GMP, non-GMO and "FDA registered facility" badges. Those are manufacturing and sourcing claims, not independent testing, a point we come back to below. Zero Carb comes in eleven flavors, from Chocolate Milkshake and Unicorn Vanilla Milkshake to Cinnamon Roll, Blueberry Donut, Iced Oatmeal Cookie and Peach Cobbler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="iso-whey" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Is in Ketogenics Iso-Whey Whey Protein Isolate Keto Friendly Protein Powder?
          </h2>

          <p>
            KetoGenics ISO-WHEY is a 100% whey protein isolate with 25 g of protein per scoop, per the label at the time of writing, and in the flavored version we read it is sweetened with sucralose rather than stevia. The store calls it "ISO-WHEY Whey Protein Isolate, Keto Friendly Protein Powder", which is why searchers type the long name. It is the brand's higher-protein option, built for people who care more about grams per scoop than about a stevia-only label.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 31 g scoop of Chocolate Cereal Milk gives 115 calories, 0.5 g of fat, 15 mg of cholesterol, 150 mg of sodium, 1.5 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of fiber, less than 1 g of total sugars and 0 g of added sugar, with 24 servings per bag. The printed ingredient list is whey protein isolate, natural and artificial flavors, sunflower lecithin, cocoa, sea salt and sucralose. Milk is the only declared allergen.
          </p>

          <p>
            That label differs from the product page in two ways. The page lists "natural flavors" and "no aspartame" among the features, which is true as far as aspartame goes, but the Chocolate Cereal Milk panel shows artificial flavors and sucralose. The page also quotes "1 to 1.5 net carbs" and 110 to 115 calories, which matches the label better. If you avoid sucralose, read the back-panel photo for your flavor on the KetoShop product page before ordering, because sweeteners can vary by flavor.
          </p>

          <p>
            The exception is Unflavored/Unsweetened ISO-WHEY. Per its label, a 27.8 g scoop gives 25 g of protein, 100 calories, 0 g of fat, 0 g of carbohydrate and 55 mg of sodium, and the only ingredients are whey protein isolate and sunflower lecithin. At about 90% protein by weight, that is the most protein-dense product KetoGenics sells, and it suits people who flavor their own shakes or want a neutral base for baking.
          </p>

          <p>
            ISO-WHEY comes in eleven flavors, including Peanut Butter Cookie, Cold Brew Coffee, Strawberry Shortcake, Fruity Cereal, Cinnamon Cereal Milk, Maple Syrup French Toast and Pumpkin Spice Glazed Donut. KetoGenics says the whey is cold-processed with microfiltration and ultrafiltration and is non-denatured. Those are processing claims we can't verify independently, and they don't change the numbers on the panel, which is what matters for macros.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Ketogenics Protein Powder Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            No: KetoGenics does not say its protein powders are third-party tested, and we found no certification for them from NSF, Informed Choice, Informed Sport, BSCG or the Clean Label Project at the time of writing. The product pages and labels mention GMP manufacturing, non-GMO ingredients and an FDA-registered facility, and the bags say "Formulated in the U.S.A." None of those is an independent check of what is in the bag.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference matters. GMP (good manufacturing practice) is the baseline set of rules every US supplement maker is supposed to follow. "FDA registered" means the facility is on the FDA's list, not that the FDA has approved or tested the product. A third-party certification like NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Choice means an outside lab tests batches for banned substances, and for NSF also checks that the label matches the contents. KetoGenics publishes no certificates of analysis on its product pages either.
          </p>

          <p>
            The labels also show that the product is "manufactured for" KetoGenics Inc. of Mahopac, New York, which means another company makes it under contract. That is normal across the supplement industry and not a red flag in itself, but it does mean you are trusting the contract manufacturer's quality controls without public test results.
          </p>

          <p>
            For most people on keto who simply want a low-carb shake, the lack of certification is a mild concern rather than a deal-breaker. For drug-tested athletes, anyone worried about heavy metals, or anyone who wants proof the protein figure is accurate, it is a reason to choose a certified powder instead. <a href='/dymatize-protein-powder-review/'>Dymatize ISO100</a> carries the Informed Choice mark, per Dymatize, with 1 to 2 g of carbs per scoop. Our guide to <a href='/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/'>protein powders without heavy metals</a> lists the products with published contaminant results.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Badges are not testing.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">GMP, non-GMO and FDA-registered badges describe how and where a product is made. Only an outside certifier such as NSF or Informed Choice, or a published lab report, tells you a batch was checked.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="zero-carb-keto" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Is Ketogenics Zero Carb Really Zero Carb and Keto Friendly?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, as far as labels go: KetoGenics Zero Carb lists 0 g of carbohydrate, 0 g of sugar and 0 g of sugar alcohol per scoop, which fits easily into a strict keto diet. US labeling rules let a food declare 0 g when a serving has less than 0.5 g, so a scoop may hold a trace of carbohydrate from the milk proteins and cocoa. Even if it did contain just under half a gram, two shakes a day would use about 1 g of a typical 20 to 50 g daily keto budget.
          </p>

          <p>
            The zero figure is believable for this kind of formula. Whey isolate is filtered until most of the lactose and fat are removed, and micellar casein is similarly low in lactose. Stevia adds no carbohydrate at the amounts used for sweetening. Compare that with many "keto" shakes that rely on fiber or sugar alcohols to advertise low net carbs while their total carbohydrate is several grams higher.
          </p>

          <p>
            ISO-WHEY is a little different. The flavored scoop we read lists 1.5 g of total carbohydrate and under 1 g of sugar, so it is low carb rather than zero carb. That is still trivial for most keto dieters, and Unflavored ISO-WHEY lists 0 g. If you count every gram, Zero Carb or Unflavored ISO-WHEY are the two to pick.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What about insulin and blood sugar?</h3>

          <p>
            Protein itself causes some insulin release, and whey does so more than most proteins. That doesn't break ketosis for most people at normal serving sizes, but it is worth knowing if you are using keto for medical reasons. One buyer on the brand's review page says she was recently diagnosed with type 2 diabetes and that the shake doesn't spike her blood sugar. That is a single report, not a study. If you have diabetes, take glucose-lowering medication, or have kidney disease, talk to your doctor or a registered dietitian before adding a protein powder. Our guide to the <a href='/best-protein-powder-for-diabetics/'>best protein powder for diabetics</a> covers what to look for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lactose-free claim also deserves a caveat. Whey isolate and casein both start as milk, and "lactose free" on a protein powder usually means very low lactose rather than none at all. Most people with lactose intolerance handle isolate well, but anyone with a milk allergy should avoid both KetoGenics dairy powders entirely. The brand's Iso Plant line, a pea and rice protein blend, is its dairy-free option.
          </p>
        </div>

