        <div id="buyers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What Do Buyers Say About Ketogenics Taste and Mixing?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyers rate KetoGenics very highly on its own site: Zero Carb shows a 4.95 average from 816 reviews and ISO-WHEY a 4.99 average from 462 reviews on the brand's review widget at the time of writing. Every review shown is four or five stars. Those are reviews hosted by the brand, though, so treat them as a sign that regular customers are happy, not as an independent rating. There are no Amazon reviews to compare against because the products aren't sold there.
          </p>

          <p>
            The praise is consistent. Buyers report that the powders mix easily, taste sweet enough to satisfy a sweet tooth, and feel creamy rather than gritty. Several reviewers mix Zero Carb with unsweetened almond milk or other plant milks, add it to coffee for flavor and creaminess, or blend it with ice into a smoothie. ISO-WHEY reviewers single out Peanut Butter Cookie and Fruity Cereal, and one with a sensitive stomach reports no digestive trouble.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which flavor is best?</h3>

          <p>
            Among the reviews we read, Zero Carb's Strawberry Milk and Iced Oatmeal Cookie get named often, and Chocolate Milkshake is the brand's lead image. For ISO-WHEY, buyers mention Peanut Butter Cookie, Fruity Cereal and Unflavored for baking. Taste is personal, and we have not tasted these ourselves, which is exactly why the sample packs are worth considering before a full bag.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Common complaints</h3>

          <p>
            The complaints are less about the powder than about buying it. The biggest is availability: KetoGenics is not on Amazon, so you pay shipping and wait for a direct order. The second is flavor strength. KetoGenics itself says the stevia-sweetened Zero Carb has a "much lighter and more mild flavor" than artificially sweetened powders, so buyers used to sucralose-heavy shakes may find it muted, especially mixed with water. The brand's tip is to use 10 to 12 oz of very cold almond milk instead of water. The third is protein per scoop: 22 g in Zero Carb is a few grams short of most isolates.
          </p>
