        <div id="vs-isopure" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Ketogenics vs Isopure Zero Carb: Which Keto Protein Is Better?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Isopure Zero Carb has more protein per scoop and is easier to buy, while KetoGenics Zero Carb has the cleaner, stevia-only label and costs a little less per gram at the prices we saw. Both list 0 g of carbohydrate per scoop, per their labels. Isopure gives 25 g of protein from pure whey isolate for about 110 calories in Creamy Vanilla; KetoGenics gives 22 g from whey isolate and casein for 100 calories.
          </p>

          <p>
            The sweetener is the clearest split. Isopure uses sucralose, and KetoGenics Zero Carb uses only stevia. People who avoid artificial sweeteners will prefer KetoGenics; people who find stevia bitter will prefer Isopure. Isopure also adds a vitamin blend, which KetoGenics doesn't. Neither carries an NSF or Informed certification in our database. Our <a href='/isopure-protein-powder-review/'>Isopure review</a> and the <a href='/protein-powder/isopure-zero-carb/'>Isopure Zero Carb label page</a> go further, and the <a href='/protein-powder-comparison/'>side-by-side compare tool</a> lines up labels for any two products in our database.
          </p>

          <p>
            On price, the 1 lb Isopure bag we saw on Amazon works out to about $2.33 per 25 g, against about $2.03 for KetoGenics before shipping. Larger Isopure tubs cost less per gram and ship free with Prime, so for regular buyers the two end up close. If you want a third-party tested option instead of either, <a href='/dymatize-protein-powder-review/'>Dymatize ISO100</a> is the closest, at about $2.12 per 25 g in the 20-serving tub.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant-based keto dieters have a different set of choices. KetoGenics' own Iso Plant blends pea and rice protein with stevia and lists 21 to 23 g of protein per scoop and about 1 net carb, per the brand. Today's <a href='/aloha-protein-powder-review/'>Aloha protein powder review</a> covers another plant blend popular with low-carb eaters, and our <a href='/best-unflavored-protein-powder/'>unflavored protein powder</a> guide lists the plain options that suit keto baking.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Ketogenics, and Who Should Skip It?
          </h2>

          <p>
            KetoGenics Zero Carb is a good buy for keto and low-carb dieters who want a stevia-sweetened, zero-carb shake they can also bake with, and who are happy to order from the brand's site. The label is short, the macros are as lean as protein powders get, and the price per gram is fair for a whey isolate blend. If the casein-thickened texture and dessert flavors appeal, it is one of the better-designed keto proteins we have looked at on paper.
          </p>

          <p>
            ISO-WHEY makes more sense for people who mainly drink protein after training and want 25 g per scoop. Choose Unflavored ISO-WHEY if you want zero carbs, no sweetener and a two-ingredient label; choose a flavored one only if sucralose doesn't bother you. Either way, the cost per 25 g lands within a few cents of Zero Carb.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip KetoGenics if you are a drug-tested athlete or simply want proof of what is in the bag, since it has no third-party certification; Dymatize ISO100 is the obvious swap. Skip it if you want Prime shipping or store pickup, since it isn't on Amazon or in stores as far as we could find. If you'd rather buy protein on a pharmacy run, our <a href='/walgreens-protein-powder/'>Walgreens protein powder</a> guide covers what is on the shelf there. And skip both dairy powders if you have a milk allergy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, a health note. Protein powder is food, but people with kidney disease, those who are pregnant or breastfeeding, older adults with medical conditions, and anyone on medication for diabetes should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first, especially before combining a high-protein shake with a strict ketogenic diet. KetoGenics' own label warns that pregnant or nursing women, people under 18 and anyone with a known medical condition should consult a physician.
          </p>
        </div>

